{
  "kanda": "aranya",
  "sarga": 62,
  "title": "Rama laments for Seetha [contd.]",
  "sourceUrl": "https://www.valmikiramayan.net/utf8/aranya/sarga62/aranyasans62.htm",
  "romanUrl": "https://www.valmikiramayan.net/utf8/aranya/sarga62/aranyaitrans62.htm",
  "translator": "Desiraju Hanumanta Rao",
  "introduction": [
    "Rama bewails for Seetha as an ambivert, ambivalent romantic epical hero and asks Lakshmana to return to Ayodhya, as Rama is certain to perish without Seetha. He thinks his agony aloud, weighing pros and cons of his situation."
  ],
  "closing": [
    "इति वाल्मीकि रामायणे आदि काव्ये अरण्य काण्डे द्वि षष्टितमः सर्गः",
    "Thus, this is the 62nd chapter in Aranya Kanda of Valmiki Ramayana, the first Epic poem of India"
  ],
  "verses": [
    {
      "id": "3.62.1",
      "label": "3.62.1",
      "numbers": [
        1
      ],
      "devanagari": "सीताम् अपश्यन् धर्मात्मा शोक उपहत चेतनः |\nविललाप महाबाहू रामः कमल लोचनः",
      "iast": "sītām apaśyan dharmātmā śoka upahata cetanaḥ |\nvilalāpa mahābāhū rāmaḥ kamala locanaḥ",
      "translation": "In not seeing Seetha that virtue-souled Rama's sagacity is marred by his anguish and he bewailed with his lotus-like eyes that are reddening like a pair of lotuses, raising is long arms.",
      "notes": [
        "Some mms use the word <em>kāma</em> in the compound <em>śoka upahata cetanaḥ</em> thus it becomes <em>kāma upahata cetana</em> and then it means 'his sagacity is marred by desire for Seetha...' and because of the adjacency of word <em>dharmātmā</em> 'righteous one...' 'a righteous desire is no sin to desire for... hence his desire for Seetha is righteous...' Then <em>mahābāhū</em> denotes that he is wailing raising his long arms, and <em>kamala locana</em> is indicative of redness of lotuses, thus his eyes are reddening by his wailing."
      ],
      "glosses": [
        {
